<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!-- One agent for every game. Replaces com.game-host.{railbaron,acquire,
	     marcopolo}.plist, which differed only in label, script and log path —
	     there is one process now, so there is one of these.

	     Paths go through the /opt/homebrew/etc/game-host symlink (created in
	     README setup) so no username or clone location is baked in. That
	     symlink outlives the Caddyfile's menu root, which used it too and is
	     gone: the composed host serves its own menu. -->
	<key>Label</key>
	<string>com.game-host</string>
	<key>ProgramArguments</key>
	<array>
		<string>/bin/sh</string>
		<string>/opt/homebrew/etc/game-host/start-host.sh</string>
	</array>
	<!-- launchd gives agents a bare PATH. node comes first from the repo's
	     node-bin symlink (created in README setup, pointing at fnm's default
	     alias) — NOT /usr/local/bin, where a leftover x86_64 node once broke
	     every native binding (esbuild, rollup) under Rosetta, 2026-08-17.

	     This is now what runs the server itself: start-host.sh execs `node`
	     on a prebuilt bundle rather than going through npm, so the PATH here
	     decides which node serves three games. It still needs esbuild and
	     rollup working for the one case where the script builds — a clone
	     with no artifact yet. -->
	<key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
	<dict>
		<key>PATH</key>
		<string>/opt/homebrew/etc/game-host/node-bin:/opt/homebrew/bin:/usr/bin:/bin</string>
	</dict>
	<key>RunAtLoad</key>
	<true/>
	<!-- Restart on crash, not on clean exit — so `launchctl bootout` (and a
	     deliberate stop) stays stopped, but a wedged process comes back.

	     This matters more than it did with three agents: a crash now takes
	     every game down at once, so the restart is the only thing standing
	     between one bad payload and an evening ending. The per-game error
	     boundary (packages/host/guard.ts) is what makes that rare; this is
	     what covers the rest.

	     Verified 2026-08-20 under a scratch agent rather than assumed: a
	     SIGKILL or a non-zero exit brings it back, and `bootout` does not.
	     A SIGTERM also does not, and that is not a hole to plug —
	     `bootout` stops a service by sending SIGTERM, main.ts handles it by
	     draining and exiting 0, and nothing can distinguish that from a
	     stray `kill` without breaking the deliberate stop. What did change
	     is that there is no longer an npm and a tsx between launchd and the
	     server: under the old chain a `pkill -f "tsx apps/host/main.ts"`
	     aimed at a worktree matched the agent's wrapper, which exited 0, and
	     the front door served 502 until somebody noticed. -->
	<key>KeepAlive</key>
	<dict>
		<key>SuccessfulExit</key>
		<false/>
	</dict>
	<key>StandardOutPath</key>
	<string>/opt/homebrew/var/log/game-host.log</string>
	<key>StandardErrorPath</key>
	<string>/opt/homebrew/var/log/game-host.log</string>
</dict>
</plist>
